# Sunder a caryatid column's own long sword with a battleaxe, and read the
# BATTLEAXE's description page before and after (bd inc-m2zi).
#
#   INCURSION_OPTIONS=tools/fixtures/options-2026-08-22.dat \
#       tools/headless.sh tools/keys/dequ-sunder.keys 5
#
# WHAT IS BEING MEASURED. A_SUND puts the VICTIM's weapon in e.EItem2, which is
# correct -- that is what sunder attacks. The response-attack loop copied
# e.EItem2 forward into the A_DEQU retaliation, so sundering an ARMED A_DEQU
# monster made its retaliation land on ITS OWN weapon and the striker's
# equipment went free. The retaliation now refuses any item the responder owns
# and falls back to the striker's weapon.
#
# WHY A CARYATID COLUMN. It carries A_DEQU for 12d6 AD_SHAT and a cursed long
# sword as gear (lib/mon3.irh:1646-1662), so it is the one monster in lib that
# is both armed and equipment-destroying. Its armour is 20 and it regenerates,
# so a first-level character cannot kill it by accident.
#
# WHY A MAUL. Both weapons in this fight would otherwise be called "long
# sword" and no oracle could tell them apart. The maul is iron, like the
# column's sword, so nothing but the name differs where it matters. It is also
# the heaviest ordinary weapon in lib and therefore the one with the most hit
# points: 12d6 against hardness 10 averages 32 points, and a battleaxe (26 hit
# points) was measured being destroyed outright by the first retaliation, which
# left no page to read.
#
# THE ORACLE is the battleaxe's own description page, reached from the
# inventory by selecting the Weapon Hand ('b') and pressing 'x'. Under the old
# behaviour it is untouched, because the column shattered its own sword
# instead.
#
# THE PAGE IS DUMPED AFTER EVERY BLOW, so that a run in which the maul is
# destroyed still leaves one readable page behind.

@include dequ-setup.keys

w
@choose "Item Acquisition"
RIGHT*9                 # walk the glyph row to ordinary weapons
@until "Maul" DOWN
DOWN                    # the wanted row is the middle of three; step onto it
ENTER
b                       # Weapon Hand -- the slot lettering skips 'd' (Drop)
SPACE                   # swap the maul into it
@while "two-handed" y
s                       # stow the starting knife the swap pushed into the air
@until "SPACE:Swap" i    # the inventory manager, if it is not already open
@until ">>b)Weapon" b    # select the Weapon Hand
@until "base value of" x # Examine: the item's own description page
@dump:sunder-before
@until "Things in View" ESC

w
@choose "Monster Summoning"
@choose "Constructs"
@choose "caryatid column"
RIGHT ENTER
@dump:sunder-placed

# 'c' opens the Tactical Options menu; Sunder Weapon then asks for a target and
# a direction key answers it.
c
@choose "Sunder Weapon"
RIGHT
@while "Attack the" y
@dump:sunder-strike-1
@until "SPACE:Swap" i
@until ">>b)Weapon" b
@until "base value of" x
@dump:sunder-after-1
@until "Things in View" ESC

c
@choose "Sunder Weapon"
RIGHT
@while "Attack the" y
@dump:sunder-strike-2
@until "SPACE:Swap" i
@until ">>b)Weapon" b
@until "base value of" x
@dump:sunder-after-2
@until "Things in View" ESC
@quit
